WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.490
YUFENG GUO: On this
episode of AI Adventures,

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:05.250
find out what Kaggle
Kernels are and how to get

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.720
started using them.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.080
Though there's no
popcorn in this episode,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.920
I can assure you that
Kaggle Kernels are popping.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.280
Kaggle is a platform for doing
and sharing data science.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.810
You may have heard about
some of their competitions,

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:21.780
which often have cash prizes.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.270
It's also a great place
to practice data science

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.390
and learn from the community.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:29.540
Kaggle Kernels are
essentially Jupyter Notebooks

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:32.540
in the browser that can be
run right before your eyes,

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:33.645
all free of charge.

00:00:33.645 --> 00:00:36.020
Let me say that again in case
you missed it, because this

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.180
is truly quite amazing.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:40.940
Kaggle Kernels is
a free platform

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.910
to run Jupyter Notebooks
in your browser.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.740
This means that you
can save yourself

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:48.140
the hassle of setting
up a local environment

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:50.230
and have a Jupyter
Notebook environment

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:52.550
right inside your browser
anywhere in the world

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:54.770
that you have an
internet connection.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:57.620
Not only that-- the processing
power for the notebook

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:02.010
comes from servers up in the
clouds, not your local machine.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.760
So you can do a lot of data
science and machine learning

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.870
without heating up your laptop.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:09.930
Kaggle also recently
upgraded all their kernels

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.500
to have more compute
power and more memory,

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.080
as well as extending
the length of time

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:19.930
that you can run a notebook
cell to up to 60 minutes.

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:20.810
But OK.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:23.200
Enough of me gushing
about Kaggle Kernels.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.120
Let's see what it
actually looks like.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.790
Once we create an
account at Kaggle.com,

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:29.500
we can choose a
dataset that we want

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.950
to play with and spin up a
new kernel or notebook in just

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:34.420
a few clicks.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.120
The dataset that we
started in comes preloaded

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:39.070
in the environment
of that kernel,

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:41.410
so there's no need
to deal with pushing

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:44.860
a dataset into that machine
or waiting for large datasets

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:46.530
to copy over a network.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.790
Of course, you can still
load additional files

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:50.750
into the kernel if you want.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:52.660
In our case, we'll
continue to play

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:54.790
with our fashion
and this dataset.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:58.240
It's a dataset that contains
10 categories of clothing

00:01:58.240 --> 00:01:59.530
and accessory types--

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:03.370
things like pants, bags,
heels, shirts, and so on.

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:06.400
There are 50,000 training
samples and 10,000 evaluation

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:07.270
samples.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:11.170
Let's explore the dataset
in our Kaggle Kernel.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:13.480
Looking at the dataset,
it's provided on Kaggle

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.610
in the form of CSV files.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:20.080
The original data was in a 28
by 28 pixel grayscale images

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.700
and they've been flattened to
become 784 distinct columns

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.710
in the CSV file.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:28.930
The file also contains
a column representing

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:32.770
the index, 0 through 9,
of that fashion item.

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:36.145
Since the dataset is already
in the environment, in pandas--

00:02:36.145 --> 00:02:37.780
this is already loaded--

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:43.260
let's use it to read these CSV
files into panda's data frames.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.540
Now that we've loaded the
data into a data frame,

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.690
we can take advantage
of all the features

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:49.670
that this brings,
which we covered

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:51.120
in the previous episode.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.020
We'll display the first
five rows with Head,

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.240
and we can run
Describe to learn more

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.940
about the structure
of the dataset.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.800
Additionally, it would
be good to visualize

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.450
some of these
images so that they

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:06.350
can have more meaning to us than
just rows upon rows of numbers.

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:10.730
Let's use matplotlib to see what
some of these images look like.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:14.450
Here we'll use the
matplotlib.pyplot library--

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:17.120
typically imported as PLT--

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:21.420
to display the arrays of
pixel values as images.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.900
We can see that these
images, while fuzzy,

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.330
are indeed still
recognizable as the clothing

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:28.880
and accessory items
that they claim to be.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:30.560
I really like that
Kaggle Kernels

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.580
lets me visualize my data in
addition to just processing it.

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.680
So Kaggle Kernels
allows us to work

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:39.590
in a fully interactive notebook
environment in the browser

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:41.256
with little to no setup.

00:03:41.256 --> 00:03:43.130
And I really want to
emphasize that we didn't

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:46.070
have to do any sort of Python
environment configuration

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:49.400
or installation of libraries,
which is really cool.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.370
Thanks for watching this
episode of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:54.020
Be sure to subscribe
to the channel

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:57.050
to catch future episodes
as they come out.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:58.520
Now what are you waiting for?

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:01.730
Head on over to Kaggle.com
and sign up for an account

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:04.250
to play with kernels today.

